---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---
<Layout>
<main id="main">
<section class="hero" id="top">
  <div class="container hero-grid">
    <div class="hero-copy fade-in">
      <p class="eyebrow">Open source · Reasoning kept in Git</p>
      <h1>Never explain<br /> the same<br /> <em>decision</em> twice.</h1>
      <p class="hero-body">Keep the why behind your code. dira records decisions and rejected alternatives in your repo, brings them into your coding agent's next session, and checks proposed work against what you already decided.</p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="btn-primary" href="#install">Install dira</a>
        <a class="link-underline" href="/why/"><u>Explore the real ledger</u></a>
      </div>
      <div class="cmd-row">
        <code>brew install kazi-org/tap/dira</code>
        <button class="copy-btn" data-copy="brew install kazi-org/tap/dira">Copy</button>
      </div>
      <p class="cmd-note">Local CLI. Plain files. No Dira account.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="hero-specimen fade-in">
      <div class="specimen">
        <div class="rail" aria-hidden="true"><span>1</span><span>2</span><span>3</span><span>4</span></div>
        <figure class="specimen-panel" >
          <figcaption class="specimen-head">A decision, with its reasons</figcaption>
          <div class="spec-row">
            <span class="spec-key">Decision</span>
            <span class="spec-val">“Use Go for dira's CLI.”</span>
            <span class="spec-meta">dec-0001 · <span class="status">Accepted</span></span>
          </div>
          <div class="spec-row">
            <span class="spec-key">Rejected alternative</span>
            <span class="spec-val">“Elixir / OTP”</span>
            <span class="spec-meta"></span>
          </div>
          <div class="spec-row">
            <span class="spec-key">Reason</span>
            <span class="spec-val">“dira runs in the path of a human waiting for a prompt. Process startup matters.”</span>
            <span class="spec-meta"></span>
          </div>
          <div class="spec-row">
            <span class="spec-key">Revisit if</span>
            <span class="spec-val">“dira grows a genuinely long-lived component.”</span>
            <span class="spec-meta"></span>
          </div>
          <div class="specimen-foot">Adapted from dira's own ledger · <a href="https://github.com/kazi-org/dira/blob/main/.dira/entries/dec-0001.md">view the actual record</a></div>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <div class="check-panel">
        <div class="check-cmd">dira check "rewrite dira in Elixir using OTP"</div>
        <h3>Conflict found: dec-0001</h3>
        <p>This proposal matches a rejected alternative. Review the recorded reason, revise the plan, or explicitly supersede the decision.</p>
        <p class="caption">Illustrative rendering of the documented check. Matching is lexical.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="how-it-works">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="section-id">01 / Continuity</p>
    <h2>The reasoning survives the session.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Capture the decision, bring it back when work resumes, and consult it before repeating an old argument.</p>
    <div class="steps-grid">
      <div class="step">
        <span class="step-num">01</span>
        <h3>Capture candidates</h3>
        <p>Claude Code hooks stage possible decisions from the conversation. You review the captures; the session's skill supplies the reasoning.</p>
        <span class="cmds">sniff · distill</span>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <span class="step-num">02</span>
        <h3>Start with context</h3>
        <p>A session-start brief brings blockers, active intentions, and recent decisions into context, capped at 1,500 tokens.</p>
        <span class="cmds">brief</span>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <span class="step-num">03</span>
        <h3>Check before proceeding</h3>
        <p>Check a proposed plan against recorded decisions. When direction changes, explicitly supersede the old record.</p>
        <span class="cmds">check · supersede</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="the-record">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="section-id">02 / The Record</p>
    <h2>A choice is only half the story.</h2>
    <p class="intro">Keep the alternative, the reason it lost, and the condition that would make it worth considering again.</p>
    <div class="record-grid">
      <div class="record-copy">
        <div class="record-controls" role="group" aria-label="Highlight a part of the record">
          <button data-part="decision" aria-pressed="false">Decision</button>
          <button data-part="rejected" aria-pressed="true">Why rejected</button>
          <button data-part="revisit" aria-pressed="false">When to revisit</button>
        </div>
        <p>Below is the same example record from dira's ledger. Selecting a part changes emphasis only — the full record stays visible.</p>
        <p><a class="link-underline" href="https://github.com/kazi-org/dira/blob/main/.dira/entries/dec-0001.md"><u>Read the original decision on GitHub</u></a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="record-demo">
        <div class="record-panel" id="record-panel">
          <div class="spec-row" data-part="decision">
            <span class="spec-key">Decision</span>
            <span class="spec-val">“Use Go for dira's CLI.”</span>
            <span class="spec-meta">dec-0001 · <span class="status">Accepted</span></span>
          </div>
          <div class="spec-row active" data-part="rejected">
            <span class="spec-key">Rejected alternative</span>
            <span class="spec-val">“Elixir / OTP” — process startup matters for a short-lived CLI in the prompt’s latency path.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="spec-row" data-part="revisit">
            <span class="spec-key">Revisit if</span>
            <span class="spec-val">“dira grows a genuinely long-lived component.”</span>
            <span class="spec-meta"></span>
          </div>
          <p class="record-explain" id="record-explain" aria-live="polite">A rejected alternative carries a reason, so a later session can examine the argument that settled it.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="your-repository">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="section-id">03 / Your Repository</p>
    <h2>The record lives with the work.</h2>
    <div class="repo-grid">
      <div class="repo-files" aria-label="Example ledger files">
        <div class="file-row"><span>.dira/config.toml</span><span class="quiet">configuration</span></div>
        <div class="file-row"><span>.dira/entries/int-0001.md</span><span class="quiet">an intent</span></div>
        <div class="file-row"><span>.dira/entries/dec-0001.md</span><span class="quiet">a decision</span></div>
        <div class="file-row"><span>.dira/cache/</span><span class="quiet">derived, rebuildable data</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="repo-copy">
        <p>Each entry is Markdown with YAML frontmatter. Commit it alongside your code. Review it in a diff. The files remain readable even without dira.</p>
        <div class="edit-row">
          <h3>Already have ADRs?</h3>
          <p>Run <code class="mono">dira import DIR</code> to inspect what your documents contain before importing. Reasoned rejections become staged entries after confirmation; documents without them can be indexed instead.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="edit-row">
          <h3>Using <a href="https://github.com/kazi-org/kazi">kazi</a>?</h3>
          <p>dira keeps the reasoning behind a goal. kazi checks whether the work satisfies its acceptance predicates. Use either independently, or keep direction and execution connected.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="install">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="section-id">04 / Installation</p>
    <h2>Give the next session a starting point.</h2>
    <div class="install-steps">
      <div class="install-step">
        <span class="step-num">1</span>
        <h3>Install the CLI</h3>
        <div class="cmd-block">
<pre>brew install kazi-org/tap/dira
dira --version</pre>
          <button class="copy-btn" data-copy="brew install kazi-org/tap/dira
dira --version">Copy</button>
        </div>
        <p>Homebrew support is documented for Apple Silicon Macs, Intel Macs, and Linux amd64. For other published builds, use the <a href="https://github.com/kazi-org/dira/releases">release archives</a>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="install-step">
        <span class="step-num">2</span>
        <h3>Initialize your project</h3>
        <p >From your project directory, answer a short interview. Choose <code class="mono">workspace</code> to seed an intent, a constraint, and an open question:</p>
        <div class="cmd-block">
          <pre>dira init --interview</pre>
          <button class="copy-btn" data-copy="dira init --interview">Copy</button>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="install-step">
        <span class="step-num">3</span>
        <h3>Connect Claude Code</h3>
        <div class="cmd-block">
<pre>dira install-hooks
dira install-skill</pre>
          <button class="copy-btn" data-copy="dira install-hooks
dira install-skill">Copy</button>
        </div>
        <p>These commands add dira's hooks and capture skill to your Claude Code configuration. <a href="https://github.com/kazi-org/dira/blob/main/README.md">Start a new session after setup.</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="install-links">
      <a class="link-underline" href="/docs/"><u>Read the command reference</u></a>
      <a class="link-underline" href="https://github.com/kazi-org/dira/releases"><u>Download release archives</u></a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="faq">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="section-id">05 / Questions</p>
    <h2>Straight answers.</h2>
    <div class="faq-list">
      <details>
        <summary>Does dira require a hosted service?</summary>
        <p>No. The CLI and ledger work locally without a Dira account. The optional web viewer runs on your machine.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Does it call an AI model?</summary>
        <p>The binary has no model client. Its semantic capture skill uses the coding-agent session you already have running.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Will it catch every contradiction?</summary>
        <p>No. <code class="mono">dira check</code> uses lexical matching against recorded decisions. It provides a concrete check with cited reasoning, not a guarantee of complete semantic detection.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Does confirming a capture accept the decision?</summary>
        <p>No. Confirmation marks the capture for semantic extraction; it stays staged until its reasoning is supplied. The agent must complete that step. <a href="/guide/#capture">Read the capture workflow.</a></p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
<section id="explore"><div class="container"><p class="section-id">06 / Go further</p><h2>Follow the reasoning.</h2><div class="resource-grid"><article class="resource"><h3><a href="/guide/">Your first decision</a></h3><p>A complete walkthrough: create a ledger, record a choice, check a plan, and change direction.</p></article><article class="resource"><h3><a href="/docs/">The command reference</a></h3><p>Every command and its flags, generated from the actual CLI at build time.</p></article><article class="resource"><h3><a href="/why/">Dira’s own ledger</a></h3><p>Explore the real intents, constraints, and decisions behind the project you’re looking at.</p></article></div></div></section></main>
</Layout>
